    eyebrow="Other sightings"
    heading="Seen <em>elsewhere.</em>"
    deck="Interviews, press, and posts from around the web, for anyone who'd like a second witness (Scrooge didn't trust his own eyes either)."
  />

  <!-- ================= STAVE FIVE: THE END OF IT ================= -->
  <section class="ac-morning" id="stave-five" aria-labelledby="ac-s5-title">
    <div class="ac-wrap ac-morning-grid">
      <figure class="ac-fig ac-fig-morning">
        <img
          src={`${IMG}/morning.jpg`}
          alt="On a bright, snowy Victorian morning, Edward as Marley's Ghost leans out of an upstairs window of a brick townhouse in his blue fedora, grinning and waving. His chain hangs over the windowsill and down the wall with floppy disks, padlocks, and a beige keyboard dangling from it, and a boy in a flat cap looks up at him from the street."
          width="1600"
          height="1195"
          loading="lazy"
          decoding="async"
        />
        <figcaption>The boy has questions. Most of them are about the keyboard.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <div class="ac-morning-body">
        <p class="ac-eyebrow ac-eyebrow-morning">Stave Five | The End of It</p>
        <h2 class="ac-h2 ac-h2-morning ac-h2-quote" id="ac-s5-title">&ldquo;What's to<span class="ac-hy">-</span>day?&rdquo;</h2>
        <p class="ac-copy ac-copy-morning">
          Scrooge wakes up, throws open the window, and asks a boy in the street what day it is, because he's lost
          track. It's Christmas Day, and &ldquo;the Spirits have done it all in one night.&rdquo; (Marley, technically,
          doesn't get a Stave Five. He's back out wandering by the end of Stave One. I gave myself a window anyway,
          because it's my website.)
        </p>
        <p class="ac-copy ac-copy-morning">
          This website did its own version of that today. A machine looked up what opened this weekend, picked
          Dickens, rewrote the page you're reading, drew me as a dead Victorian 7 times (fedora on every one, per
          standing orders), ran the tests, and shipped it. It's the part of my &ldquo;Your Day, Run
          by Ten Agents&rdquo; talk that I don't have to bring slides for!
        </p>
        <p class="ac-copy ac-copy-morning">
          Dickens gives Scrooge's follow-through about 1 paragraph (&ldquo;He became as good a friend, as good a
          master, and as good a man, as the good old city knew&rdquo;), and that's all we hear about what must have
          been a pretty awkward January at the office. The real version takes a budget and most of a year, and people
          laughed at Scrooge at first (Dickens says he let them). A talk can do roughly what Marley did, which is get
          the &ldquo;Humbug&rdquo; to stop at the first syllable, and I'd be overselling it if I promised you the
          other 4 staves from a stage.
        </p>
        <p class="ac-copy ac-copy-morning">
          The longer version, including the deal I once lost to a set of courtside Lakers tickets, is on the blog
          today: <a href={POST}>What Actually Changed Scrooge's Mind?</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ================= THE DOOR ================= -->
  <section class="ac-section ac-door" aria-labelledby="ac-door-title">
    <div class="ac-wrap ac-door-inner">
      <p class="ac-eyebrow">Expect the first when the bell tolls One</p>
      <h2 class="ac-h2" id="ac-door-title">Is there a Scrooge at your company?</h2>
      <p class="ac-copy">
        If there is (or you're it, which is fine, he's the hero of the book), I'd be glad to be the first visitor.
        Keynotes start at $15,000, and the details are on the speaking page. If you'd rather just talk, 13 minutes on
        my calendar is free! Or write me at Edward@Roske.AI and tell me which humbug you hear the most (if it's not
        on the ledger, I clearly need to get out more).
      </p>
      <div class="ac-ctas">
        <a class="ac-btn ac-btn-main" href="/speaking/">Book the visit</a>
        <a class="ac-btn ac-btn-ghost" href={CAL} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Book 13 minutes</a>
        <a class="ac-btn ac-btn-ghost" href="/contact/">Write me</a>
      </div>
      <p class="ac-disclaimer">
        Every image of Edward on this page is AI-generated from his real headshot (he isn't dead, and the frock coat
        isn't his). The quotations are from Charles Dickens's A Christmas Carol (1843), which is in the public domain,
        checked against the Project Gutenberg text. Nothing here is affiliated with Ebenezer: A Christmas Carol,
        Paramount Pictures, or anybody in the film, and nobody from the film appears on this page. The hooded figure
        declined to comment.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style is:global>
  /* =====================================================================
     AN A.I. CAROL (2026-11-14)
     Ebenezer: A Christmas Carol week. Light mode is the 1843 first
     edition (cream paper, title-page red and blue ink); dark mode is the
     bedchamber at 1 AM (soot, candle amber, spectral green).

     FOUR blocks are pinned and look the same in both modes, so every
     color inside them is a literal: the title-page book (paper), the
     Humbug Ledger (paper), the three Spirits (night), and Stave Five
     (bright morning). Everything else reads the --ac-* vars, which flip.
     Global on purpose: [data-theme] selectors are unreliable inside
     Astro's scoped CSS. All classes ac-.
     ===================================================================== */
  :root,
  [data-theme="dark"] {
    --ac-bg: #100e0c;
    --ac-surface: #1b1713;
    --ac-band: #171310;
